## Typed FFI return surface (docs/specs/types/typed_ffi_returns.spec.md).
## `def self.new` declares `-> Mutex[T]`; the codegen still treats the
## return value as i64 at the C ABI (see `ty_to_cranelift`), so
## `ruxen_mutex_new(int64_t initial)` is called unchanged. Per B1+B3
## of the spec, T is bound from the constructor's first arg type;
## `Mutex.new(7)` reports `Mutex[Int]` at the let-binding site.
## `Mutex[T: Send]` — the payload must be `Send` to be guarded across
## threads. Feature B (generic trait-bound enforcement) checks this bound
## at the `Mutex.new(value)` construction site (E1101), replacing the old
## hardcoded `concurrency.rs` Send check.
class Mutex[T: Send]
  include Send

  include Sync

  lib "runtime/mutex.c"
    def self.new as "ruxen_mutex_new"(initial: T) -> Mutex[T]
    def lock_raw as "ruxen_mutex_lock" -> MutexGuard[T]
    def try_lock_raw as "ruxen_mutex_try_lock" -> MutexGuard[T]
    def is_poisoned as "ruxen_mutex_is_poisoned" -> Int
    def clear_poison as "ruxen_mutex_clear_poison" -> nil
    def into_inner_raw as "ruxen_mutex_into_inner" -> T
    def drop as "ruxen_mutex_drop" -> nil
  end

  ## Ergonomic poison-surfacing wrappers layered on the `_raw` FFI.
  ## `ruxen_mutex_lock` always returns the guard and sets the internal
  ## poisoned bit; these shims read that bit and shape the surface
  ## Result/Option, per the runtime comment in `runtime/mutex.c`. They
  ## were advertised by typeck (`method_resolvers/concurrency.rs`) and the
  ## `std_sync_concurrency_surface` typeck pin but had NO codegen body, so
  ## any real `m.lock` call link-failed with `Undefined symbols: _Mutex_lock`
  ## (gui-stack §Q40). Implemented here as real `.rx` bodies — the generic
  ## class monomorphizes them per `Mutex[T]` instantiation.

  ## Acquire the lock, surfacing poison as `Err(PoisonError)`.
  def lock -> Result[MutexGuard[T], PoisonError]
    let g = self.lock_raw
    if self.is_poisoned != 0
      Err(PoisonError.new)
    else
      Ok(g)
    end
  end

  ## Acquire the lock, panicking on poison (the `!` ergonomic form).
  def lock! -> MutexGuard[T]
    self.lock_raw
  end

  ## Consume the mutex, returning the inner value (poison surfaced).
  def into_inner -> Result[T, PoisonError]
    if self.is_poisoned != 0
      Err(PoisonError.new)
    else
      Ok(self.into_inner_raw)
    end
  end
end
